module gunbc.guarantee_stall.roster_re_enumerates_its_own_rows_stall

import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, StructurallyImpossible }
import gunbc.guarantee_stall { GuaranteeStall, UncountedNotEnumerable, climbs_when }
import gunbc.rung_climb { AwaitsOneGrounding, GroundingUndeclared }

// THE CURRENT RUNG IS CORRECTED TO `Mitigatable`, BECAUSE THE JOIN IT RESTED ON NEVER EXECUTES.
// Several paragraphs below say `v1_compiler.cli_run.rostered_row_join` runs inside the required-CI
// parse phase on every push. Measured when `gunbc.guarantee_stall.roster` was derived: the entry
// point `run_rostered_row_join` has no caller anywhere in the checkout, as
// `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_drops_rederivation_instrument_sits_in_the_population_it_measures`
// already records. A `MechanicallyPreventable` reading needs the gate to execute (DESIGN section
// 4b(1)), so it was inflation, and the paragraphs below are kept as the record of what was claimed.
// What does hold: for the three derived rosters an author cannot forget a row FILED IN THE ROW
// DIRECTORY, because the list has no author. A row of an enrolled type declared anywhere else is not
// in the roster and nothing refuses it. A row under a stem that is not its declaration name is
// refused by the native derivation (`v2.compiler.source_authority` `DerivedRosterRowMisplaced`) and
// reds `v2.test.claim.derived_roster_seed_agreement`, neither of which runs on the required path. The
// next climb is that join executing on a required path, and that RFM row states why it is parked
// behind a grounding (the `DeclFact` initializer projection carrying bound names, so the join is
// emitted and judged natively) rather than behind a host caller. So the blocker stays
// `AwaitsOneGrounding`, for the immediate climb this time.
//
// WHY THE BLOCKER IS `AwaitsOneGrounding` TODAY AND WAS `ClimbableButUnbuilt` BEFORE, which is the
// one thing a reader of this row is likeliest to read as a contradiction against the paragraphs
// below, since several of them were written while the earlier classification held. DESIGN section
// 4b(2) obliges a stall to separate "cannot climb further" from "can climb after one grounding"
// from "can climb NOW but unbuilt", and that classification is about THIS ROW'S STANDING STATE --
// whether it can move off the rung it sits on -- not about the last step to its ceiling. The class
// sat OUTSIDE THE LADDER and the climb off it needed no capability that did not exist, so it was
// climbable and unbuilt; that climb has now been taken (the join described further down), the row
// stands at `MechanicallyPreventable`, and the ONLY remaining climb is the one to the ceiling, which
// does await a grounding. So the blocker changed because the standing state changed, not because
// the reading of section 4b(2) did.
//
// THE DISTINCTION THAT PARAGRAPH PRESERVES IS STILL LOAD-BEARING and is why it is rewritten rather
// than deleted: while an affordable climb was outstanding, calling the blocker `AwaitsOneGrounding`
// because the CEILING needs one would have put an affordable climb behind a capability nobody is
// building, and would have asserted the third case is the second -- the misclassification caught in
// review of the first version of this row (gunbc#10366, review 59852), where the blocker
// contradicted the evidence six lines above it. The same error is available in the other direction
// now: leaving `ClimbableButUnbuilt` standing after the affordable climb landed would claim a climb
// is available today that is not.
//
// THE CEILING'S GROUNDING NOW REACHES A FIELD, AND ONLY BECAUSE THE TWO COINCIDE TODAY -- the
// carrier limit this paragraph was written to record is unchanged and is restated rather than
// deleted. `GuaranteeStall` carries ONE `blocker`, which answers for the IMMEDIATE climb, and
// `ceiling` is a bare `GuaranteeRung` with nothing attached; there is still no field in which "what
// the LAST step needs" can be written. It is legible in `blocker.grounding` at the moment only
// because the immediate climb and the last climb have become the same climb: the affordable one has
// been taken. Should this row gain another intermediate rung, the ceiling's grounding falls back out
// of every field and into this paragraph, which is why it stays here in full. It is: declaration introspection sufficient to BIND a
// discovered data declaration as a typed value of its declared type -- not merely to report that it
// exists, its name, or its declared type as strings -- such that a roster is folded over the
// declared population with no authored name per row, at which point an unrostered row has no
// constructor because there is no list to omit it from. Stated as BINDING and not as introspection
// deliberately: a trigger naming discovery would be satisfied by `data_decl_type_facts`, which
// already exists, while the capability it is meant to restore stayed dead -- the DESIGN section
// 4b(3) failure of a trigger naming less than it restores. This paragraph is not evidence that
// anything holds; no Accepted program reads it (section 4c). It is the honest home for a fact the
// type cannot carry, and if a later lane gives the ceiling its own grounding field, this is the row
// whose prose moves into it.
//
// THE TWO CAPABILITIES ARE SEPARABLE AND ONLY ONE IS MISSING, measured rather than assumed.
// MEMBERSHIP discovery EXISTS: `v2.std.decl_index` `data_decl_type_facts` returns module path,
// declaration name and declared type per top-level `data` declaration, so the declared population of
// a rostered row type is derivable today and a declared-versus-rostered identity join is affordable
// without any new capability. VALUE BINDING DOES NOT EXIST, and it is what a DERIVED roster needs:
// `decl_facts` yields `node: Node`, the initializer projection -- a syntax tree, which that module's
// own annotation records as dropping the declared type -- and there is no glob or derived import, so
// a value is bound only by an authored name. Decoding a typed value out of a data initializer is
// already declared blocked in a second carrier: `test.fixture.scaffold_disposition_census.pool_notes`
// records the same constructor/variant-value identity prerequisite as the reason nothing there
// decodes a `Disposition`. This separability is what the paragraphs above rest on: it is why the
// blocker could be `ClimbableButUnbuilt` while the ceiling awaited a grounding, and why, once
// membership discovery had been spent on the affordable climb, the value binding is the whole of
// what remains.
//
// THE DEMAND PREDATES THIS ROW, which is what keeps it from being self-serving. `std.primitives`
// `primitive_contract_roster_note` independently names this class and this dissolution for its own
// contract roster -- "derive the roster by enumerating *_contract declarations once declaration
// introspection lands, so contract surfaces read the module rather than a second list" -- and
// `gunbc.ci.ci_spec` names the same capability again, as does `std.primitive_identity`. None was
// authored for this lane.
//
// THIS ROW DEMONSTRATES ITS OWN SUBJECT ON THE WAY IN, recorded because it is evidence and not an
// embarrassment: filing it appends one import and one entry to `gunbc.guarantee_stall.roster`, which
// is one of the carriers the subject is about. The population is not theoretical.
//
// HOW THE POPULATION WAS SEARCHED, stated because a bounded population whose discovery method is
// unstated cannot be told from the carriers someone happened to grep. The executed rule over every
// non-test `.dag` in `dag/` and `src/`: a module that imports at least five declarations by name AND
// enumerates at least five of those same names again as bare entries of a list literal. It returned
// thirteen carriers, headed by this carrier's own roster and by
// `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.roster`. `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.roster` LEFT THAT SET in
// gunbc#10822: its list is derived from sibling row files, so an author files a row and does not
// write a roster entry. The double authoring this row names is gone for that member. What remains
// there is a host directory fold (`v1_compiler.cli_run.derived_row_roster`) plus the parse join
// that still refuses a skip. That is NOT this row's declared climb (declaration VALUE binding).
// It is a second path beside that climb, and it does not retire this stall. `gunbc.rung_drop.roster`
// left the set the same way, and so did THIS CARRIER'S OWN ROSTER: `gunbc.guarantee_stall.roster`
// is derived by the same host fold (`GUARANTEE_STALL`), its hand list and the physical module are
// deleted, and the three rows that were declared outside the directory moved into row files in it.
// The other carriers the proxy found still re-enumerate by hand, and none of the three derived
// rosters is value binding, so the rung and the trigger below are unchanged. TWO BLIND SPOTS ARE KNOWN AND NAMED. It UNDER-reports: the
// `std.primitives` contract roster is intra-module, so its second enumeration imports nothing and
// the rule cannot see it, and the same holds for every future roster of same-module rows. It
// OVER-reports: a list literal of imported names is not necessarily a second enumeration of a
// rostered population. So the count is a proxy over a class that gains a member whenever a carrier
// splits into per-row files, which is why the population is UNCOUNTED rather than a list of
// thirteen: rendering that list would read as "these are affected", the selection-view-read-as-
// population narrow, when the real exposure is every per-row carrier that exists or will.
//
// THE ROW MOVED OFF THE LADDER'S OUTSIDE, AND THE TRIGGER THAT MOVED IT IS DISCHARGED, RECORDED
// HERE BECAUSE THE FIELDS BELOW NOW SAY SOMETHING DIFFERENT THAN THEY DID. The declared trigger --
// a declared-versus-rostered identity join executing as a merge-blocking check on every rostered
// row type -- is BUILT AND ENROLLED BUT DOES NOT EXECUTE: `gunbc.rostered_row_join` declares the
// enrolled row types and `v1_compiler.cli_run.rostered_row_join` implements the join, but its entry
// point has no caller and no required context reaches it (corrected; see the paragraph at the top
// of this row). This paragraph once said it ran inside the required-CI parse phase on every push.
// The
// declared population is read through `v2.std.decl_index` `data_decl_type_facts` -- the same host
// walk that builtin marshals, shared rather than re-derived -- and each roster's membership is read
// out of the parse that phase has already performed, because a list literal's element NAMES are
// reachable only there.
//
// WHAT IT ESTABLISHES IS THE OMISSION GOING LOUD, AND NOTHING MORE, FOR EVERY CARRIER THAT STILL
// HAND-APPENDS. Every author of those carriers still writes the row and then writes the roster
// entry; the double authoring is untouched there, the invalid state stays writable, and safety
// would depend on this check executing, which it does not. The three derived rosters
// (`gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.roster`, `gunbc.rung_drop.roster`, `gunbc.guarantee_stall.roster`)
// are no longer in that set. Calling the RecurringFailureMode directory fold a climb of this stall
// would be inflation: the host writer is not value binding. With the join executing this would be
// `MechanicallyPreventable`; without it the reading is `Mitigatable` (top of this row).
//
// IT REFUSED FIVE ROWS ON ITS FIRST EXECUTION, inside this row's own carrier, which is the evidence
// that the population was not theoretical: five valid GuaranteeStall declarations were standing
// declared and unrostered -- two in `gunbc.namespace_wave_admission`, two in
// `gunbc.runner.runner_service_activation`, one in `gunbc.declaration_index_seed_growth` -- absent
// from every projection this roster feeds. They were rostered by hand with that change, and the
// three that survived later moved into row files of the derived roster;
// `gunbc.guarantee_stall.roster_pins` records why they were invisible and why moving them is safe.
//
// THE JOIN IS A SPELLING COMPARISON AND IT IS NAMED AS ONE. `DataDeclTypeFact` carries `type_name`
// as a `String` -- the authored head name of the declared type annotation -- so membership is
// decided by comparing spellings over a population discovered by parse. That is DISCOVERY EVIDENCE,
// not resolved typed membership: two distinct types spelled alike in different modules are one
// subject to this check, and a row whose annotation is written through an alias is invisible to it.
// It is exact today only because each enrolled spelling is declared in exactly one module, which is
// a property of the corpus and not of the check. The same missing capability bounds both halves:
// declaration introspection reports names and spellings, and binds no values.
//
// THE CEILING IS UNCHANGED AND SO IS WHAT IT NEEDS. The blocker becomes `AwaitsOneGrounding`
// because the affordable climb has been taken and the remaining one is not affordable: reaching
// `StructurallyImpossible` still requires binding a discovered data declaration as a typed VALUE of
// its declared type, so a roster is folded over the declared population with no authored name per
// row. The long annotation above states that capability in full and it is not restated here.
data roster_re_enumerates_its_own_rows_stall: GuaranteeStall = GuaranteeStall {
  subject: "a roster of per-module rows enumerates by hand the declarations it rosters, so a declared row absent from the list is silently absent from every projection derived from it",
  current: Mitigatable,
  ceiling: StructurallyImpossible,
  blocker: AwaitsOneGrounding {
    grounding: GroundingUndeclared,
  },
  population: UncountedNotEnumerable { reason: "every carrier whose rows live one per module and are re-enumerated in a roster, a class that gains a member whenever a carrier splits into per-row files; an executed proxy over non-test dag/ and src/ modules importing at least five names and re-listing at least five of them returned thirteen carriers on the derivation base named in the PR body, which under-reports intra-module rosters such as the std.primitives contract roster and over-reports any list literal of imported names, so it bounds nothing" },
  next_rung_trigger: climbs_when(capability: "the `v2.std.decl_index` `DeclFact` initializer projection carries bound names, so the declared-versus-rostered join of `gunbc.rostered_row_join` is expressed in .dag, emitted, and judged by the emitted binary on a required path, failing on any production finding (gunbc.recurring_failure_mode a_drops_rederivation_instrument_sits_in_the_population_it_measures) -- at which point a row of an enrolled type declared outside its row directory, or under a stem that is not its declaration name, refuses as DeclaredNotRostered. A host caller of run_rostered_row_join does not satisfy this. The CEILING still needs declaration introspection sufficient to BIND a discovered data declaration as a typed value of its declared type, so the roster has no list at all; that is stated in the annotation above and is not this rung's trigger")
}
